    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-346084",
      "knowledge_point_id": "92e86e5f-31cd-57ae-a142-023c816d0b33",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating the Definite Integral of a Trigonometric Function",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
