      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-(-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
